---
import { segmentButtonActiveClass } from "../../lib/button-styles";
import { getPluginNavItem } from "../../plugin-nav";
import DemoButton from "../astro/DemoButton.astro";
import DemoExample from "../DemoExample.astro";
import DemoSection from "../DemoSection.astro";

const plugin = getPluginNavItem("theme")!;

const themeBtnActive = segmentButtonActiveClass;

const PREFS_CODE = `<div x-data>
  <!-- $theme is a getter on the SAME object as $store.theme, not a second
       projection: renaming storeKey renames both. -->
  <DemoButton @click="$theme.set('light')">Light</DemoButton>
  <DemoButton @click="$theme.set('dark')">Dark</DemoButton>
  <DemoButton @click="$theme.set('system')">System</DemoButton>

  <ul>
    <li>Preference (current): <strong x-text="$theme.current"></strong></li>
    <li>System:            <strong x-text="$theme.system"></strong></li>
    <li>Resolved:          <strong x-text="$theme.resolved"></strong></li>
  </ul>
</div>

<!-- The difference between the three is only visible in 'system': current is
     your choice, system is what the OS says, and resolved is the one that got
     applied. -->`;

const TOGGLE_CODE = `<!-- toggle() decides on RESOLVED, not on the preference, and
     always writes an explicit preference. On 'system' with the OS in dark, a
     click writes 'light' — it never goes back to 'system'.

     That is why the button is labelled with resolved: in 'system' the two
     coincide, and that is exactly the case the text has to get right. -->
<DemoButton @click="$theme.toggle()">
  <span x-text="$theme.resolved === 'dark' ? 'Light' : 'Dark'">toggle()</span>
</DemoButton>

<!-- reset() is NOT an inverse toggle: it goes back to defaultTheme ('system')
     and also removes the localStorage key, even if you are already on system. -->
<DemoButton variant="ghost" @click="$theme.reset()">reset()</DemoButton>`;

const PERSIST_CODE = `<!-- The three things the package brings and a hand-rolled toggle does
     not have. None of them is visible in the three numbers above. -->

<!-- 1. Persistence. The choice is written to localStorage under the 'theme'
        key and createThemeController() reads it back in setup(), before
        applying anything. -->

<!-- 2. Anti-flash on the FIRST load. The package does NOT do that: the
        controller runs after Alpine has started, so a hard reload in dark theme
        would paint light first. An inline script in <head>, before any
        stylesheet, reading the same key, is what solves it. -->

<!-- 3. Cross-tab. The controller subscribes to the 'storage' event: open this
        page in two tabs and change the theme in one — the other follows. -->

<!-- 4. View transitions. This playground navigates with Astro's ClientRouter,
        and every swap replaces the <html> element: the server's class comes back
        and the theme would look like it resets on navigation. That is why the
        plugin is registered with reapplyEvents: ['astro:after-swap'], which
        calls apply() — the forced write that ignores the "already applied". -->`;
// ──
---

<DemoSection id={plugin.id} title={plugin.title} api={plugin.api} description={plugin.description}>
	<DemoExample
		title="Three values, not one: choice, OS and applied"
		note="A hand-rolled theme toggle has one variable. This one has three, and they only agree while you are on `system` — which is exactly when the difference matters. Use `$theme` or `$store.theme`, it is the same object."
		open
		code={PREFS_CODE}
	>
		<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-sm" data-testid="theme-state">
			<li>Preference (current): <strong x-text="$store.theme.current"></strong></li>
			<li>System: <strong x-text="$store.theme.system"></strong></li>
			<li>Resolved: <strong x-text="$store.theme.resolved"></strong></li>
		</ul>
		<div class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
			<DemoButton class={themeBtnActive} x-bind:data-active="$store.theme.current === 'light' ? 'true' : null" @click="$store.theme.set('light')">Light</DemoButton>
			<DemoButton class={themeBtnActive} x-bind:data-active="$store.theme.current === 'dark' ? 'true' : null" @click="$store.theme.set('dark')">Dark</DemoButton>
			<DemoButton class={themeBtnActive} x-bind:data-active="$store.theme.current === 'system' ? 'true' : null" @click="$store.theme.set('system')">System</DemoButton>
		</div>
		<p class="mt-3 text-xs text-[var(--ios-label-secondary)]">
			The <strong>System</strong> button stays active while the preference is
			<code>system</code>. If the OS is in dark, <code>current</code> and
			<code>resolved</code> will say <code>dark</code> too — that is the only window in
			which they look different.
		</p>
	</DemoExample>

	<DemoExample
		title="toggle() decides on what is applied, and never goes back to system"
		note="This is the API trap. Press toggle while on `system`: it does not flip the other way, it writes an explicit preference. And `reset()` is not its inverse — it goes back to the default value and removes the storage key."
		code={TOGGLE_CODE}
	>
		<div class="space-y-3">
			<div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
				<DemoButton data-testid="theme-toggle" @click="$store.theme.toggle()">
					<span x-text="$store.theme.resolved === 'dark' ? 'Light' : 'Dark'">toggle()</span>
				</DemoButton>
				<DemoButton variant="ghost" @click="$store.theme.reset()">reset()</DemoButton>
			</div>
			<p class="text-sm">
				After <code>toggle()</code>: <code>current</code> is
				<strong x-text="$store.theme.current"></strong> · after
				<code>reset()</code> it goes back to <code>system</code> and the
				<code>localStorage</code> key is removed.
			</p>
		</div>
	</DemoExample>

	<DemoExample
		title="What you do not see: persistence, anti-flash, cross-tab and the router"
		note="The three numbers above are the trivial part. Reload the page, open two tabs, navigate around the playground: that is what the package gives you, and it does not fit in a single block."
		code={PERSIST_CODE}
	>
		<div class="space-y-3 text-xs text-[var(--ios-label-secondary)]">
			<p>
				<strong>Reload the page.</strong> The three numbers above are not the
				interesting part — they are the same three variables any theme toggle
				has. What the plugin adds is everything around them:
			</p>
			<ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
				<li>
					<strong>Persistence.</strong> The choice is written to
					<code>localStorage</code> under <code>theme</code> and
					<code>setup()</code> reads it back <em>before</em> applying anything.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Anti-flash.</strong> The package does <em>not</em> do that, and it
					is worth saying so: the controller runs after Alpine has started, so a
					hard reload in dark theme would paint light first. A
					<code>&lt;script is:inline&gt;</code> in the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> of
					<code>BaseLayout.astro</code>, reading the same key before any
					stylesheet, is what solves it. That is host code.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Cross-tab sync.</strong> Open this page in two tabs and change
					the theme in one: the other follows, because the controller subscribes
					to the <code>storage</code> event. With a hand-rolled toggle you would
					read <code>localStorage</code> once at startup and never again.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>View transitions.</strong> This playground uses Astro's
					<code>ClientRouter</code>, and every swap replaces the
					<code>&lt;html&gt;</code> element — the server's class comes back and
					the theme would look like it resets on navigation. The plugin is
					registered with
					<code>reapplyEvents: ['astro:after-swap']</code>, which calls
					<code>apply()</code>: the forced write that ignores the "already
					applied". That single option is why the theme survives navigation here.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				<code>current</code> is your choice (<code>light</code> / <code>dark</code> /
				<code>system</code>), <code>system</code> is what the OS reports, and
				<code>resolved</code> is the combination — the value actually applied.
			</p>
		</div>
	</DemoExample>
</DemoSection>
